Reentrancy is the classic EVM exploit (2016 DAO hack): an attacker's contract re-enters a vulnerable function while its state is stale, draining funds repeatedly. The vulnerable pattern makes an external call BEFORE updating storage:
```solidity
function withdraw(uint256 amount) external {
    require(balances[msg.sender] >= amount);
    (bool ok, ) = msg.sender.call{value: amount}(""); // interaction FIRST
    balances[msg.sender] -= amount;                     // effect LAST
}
```
The fix is checks-effects-interactions (CEI): update balances BEFORE the external call, and/or use a reentrancy guard (nonReentrant). A malicious recipient calling back into withdraw() before balances[msg.sender] is decremented sees the old balance and withdraws again. Pattern class: Reentrancy. Severity: critical.

Before Solidity 0.8, arithmetic silently wrapped: an underflow made a small balance huge and an overflow made supply wrap. The canonical vulnerable pattern:
```solidity
// Solidity < 0.8 wraps silently
function transfer(address to, uint256 amount) external {
    balances[msg.sender] -= amount; // underflow if amount > balance
    balances[to] += amount;         // overflow wraps to small value
}
```
Solidity 0.8+ reverts on overflow by default, but unchecked blocks, unsafe casts, and non-EVM chains still wrap. Audit guidance: check arithmetic on balances/allowances and any unchecked/cast operations. Pattern class: Business-Logic / Input-Validation. Severity: high.

tx.origin returns the account that STARTED the transaction, not the immediate caller, so a phishing contract can pass an owner check made with tx.origin:
```solidity
function withdrawAll() external {
    require(tx.origin == owner); // attacker's contract passes this
    payable(owner).transfer(address(this).balance);
}
```
The victim (owner) calls the attacker's contract; the attacker's contract calls withdrawAll(); tx.origin is the victim, so the check passes and funds move to the attacker. Use msg.sender for authorization, never tx.origin except for explicit anti-phishing checks. Pattern class: Access-Control. Severity: high.

Low-level Solidity calls return a success boolean instead of reverting. Ignoring it lets a contract continue as if a transfer succeeded when it failed:
```solidity
(bool success, ) = recipient.call{value: amount}("");
// success ignored — accounting proceeds as if the payment landed
```
This corrupts balances, lets attackers complete flows that should have failed, and enables griefing. Always require(success) or handle the failure branch; prefer high-level interface calls that revert automatically. Related: transfer()/send() with fixed 2300 gas can brick payments as gas costs rise. Pattern class: Business-Logic / Input-Validation. Severity: high.
